{
 "schemaVersion": 1,
 "id": "kukhnya-s01e01-scene-06",
 "pair": "ru-en",
 "title": "Проигрыш Спартака  (Spartak's Loss)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "nastya": "Настя",
  "senya": "Сеня"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Back at Claude Monet, the kitchen is oddly silent. Nastya, a waitress, wants to know why, and Senya, one of the cooks, tells her: the chef is coming, and he is in a foul mood because of last night's football. This is the Russian first.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Почему на кухне так тихо?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Шеф идёт. Опять злой.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Что случилось? Опять футбол?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Да, вчера играл Спартак.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "И сколько он проиграл?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Сто штук.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Ничего себе! Сто тысяч рублей?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Да, ровно сто тысяч.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Они всегда проигрывают перед сменой.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "И что теперь делать?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Молчи и просто работай.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Не зли шефа сегодня.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Понятно. Буду очень осторожной.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Вот и молодец. За работу!",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now the same scene, slowly.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Почему на кухне так тихо?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Why is the kitchen so quiet?' Russian says ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "на кухне",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", \"on the kitchen\", where English says \"in\", and it's what everyone says about a restaurant kitchen. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "тихо?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is \"quiet\", for a voice or a room.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Шеф идёт. Опять злой.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Chef is coming. Angry again.' Russian covers \"is coming\" with ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "идёт.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", the on-foot kind. The second sentence has no \"is\" at all: just \"again angry\". ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Шеф",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is borrowed from French \"chef\", meaning chief.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Что случилось? Опять футбол?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What happened? Football again?' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Что случилось?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is \"what happened\", a question you'll need constantly. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "футбол?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is borrowed whole, and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Опять",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", \"again\", is the word Senya used a moment ago.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Да, вчера играл Спартак.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, yesterday Spartak played.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "вчера",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is \"yesterday\", a handy single word. Russian puts the news first, so ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "играл",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", \"played\", comes before the team. Spartak is a Moscow football club, and the chef takes it personally.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "И сколько он проиграл?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And how much did he lose?' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "проиграл?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "играл",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", played, with a prefix in front, and that prefix turns \"played\" into \"lost\". ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Сколько",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", \"how much\", is the same word you'd use to ask a price.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Сто штук.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'A hundred grand.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Сто штук.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is slang: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "сто",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is a hundred, and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "штук.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means \"a piece\", which is street slang for a thousand roubles. So it's literally \"a hundred pieces\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Ничего себе! Сто тысяч рублей?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Unbelievable! A hundred thousand rubles?' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Ничего себе!",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", \"nothing to oneself\", is an exclamation of surprise like \"wow\". After a number like a hundred thousand, the word for \"ruble\" turns into ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "рублей?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Да, ровно сто тысяч.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, exactly one hundred thousand.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "ровно",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means \"exactly\", and it also means \"even\" or \"level\". Senya adds it because the amount is the whole point.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Они всегда проигрывают перед сменой.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'They always lose before his shift.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "проигрывают",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the verb for losing stretched out in the middle, which makes it a habit: \"they keep losing\". ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "перед сменой.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is \"before the shift\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "И что теперь делать?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And what do we do now?' There's no \"we\" in Russian. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "что теперь делать?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is \"what to do\", the plain \"to do\" with nothing else, and that's how people ask for advice.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Молчи и просто работай.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Stay silent and just work.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Молчи",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "работай.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " are both commands, and both are short. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "просто",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the shrug from earlier: just work, don't make it complicated.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Не зли шефа сегодня.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Do not anger the chef today.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Не зли",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " has the word for angry inside it, which Senya used a moment ago: \"don't make him angry\". ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "шефа",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " has an -a on the end because the chef is the one being angered. And ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "сегодня.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is spelled with a g but said like a v.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Понятно. Буду очень осторожной.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Understood. I will be very careful.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Понятно.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " does the job of \"understood\". Nastya is a woman, so \"careful\" ends in the feminine, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "осторожной.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ": the \"as a\" ending.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Вот и молодец. За работу!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good job. To work!' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Вот и молодец.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is \"there you go, well done\". ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "молодец.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the praise word from the soup scene. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "За работу!",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", \"for work\", is how you say \"let's get going\". Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Почему на кухне так тихо?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Шеф идёт. Опять злой.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Что случилось? Опять футбол?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Да, вчера играл Спартак.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "И сколько он проиграл?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Сто штук.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Ничего себе! Сто тысяч рублей?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Да, ровно сто тысяч.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Они всегда проигрывают перед сменой.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "И что теперь делать?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Молчи и просто работай.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Не зли шефа сегодня.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Понятно. Буду очень осторожной.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Вот и молодец. За работу!",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "So the rule of the kitchen is simple: when the chef's team loses, you keep your head down and work. Take away the phrase for \"to work!\", because you'll hear it again.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "До встречи!",
   "gap": 0.3
